A.2.9 services.jotain.dockerfileLsp.enable
------------------------------------------

Whether to enable Dockerfile language server (‘docker-langserver’),
auto-attached by Eglot in ‘dockerfile-mode’.

   _Type:_ boolean

   _Default:_

false

   _Example:_

true

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.10 services.jotain.eca.enable
---------------------------------

Whether to install ‘~/.config/eca/config.json’ for the ‘eca’ server (the
AI pair-programming backend in lisp/init-ai.el).  Enabled automatically
when ‘services.jotain.eca.openrouter.enable’ is set or
‘services.jotain.eca.settings’ is non-empty.

   _Type:_ boolean

   _Default:_

eca.openrouter.enable || eca.settings != { }

   _Example:_

true

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.11 services.jotain.eca.environmentFile
------------------------------------------

Alias of ‘services.jotain.environmentFile’.

   _Type:_ null or absolute path

   _Default:_

null

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.12 services.jotain.eca.openrouter.enable
--------------------------------------------

Whether to enable the default OpenRouter provider in the generated ‘eca’
config.  The provider and its model catalogue come from
‘config/eca/config.json’ (kept in sync with gptel's models in
lisp/init-ai.el); the API key is read at runtime from
‘OPENROUTER_API_KEY’ via eca's ‘${env:...}’ interpolation, so no secret
is written to the Nix store.  Supply the key through
‘services.jotain.environmentFile’.  gptel defaults to OpenRouter
regardless of this option .

   _Type:_ boolean

   _Default:_

false

   _Example:_

true

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.13 services.jotain.eca.settings
-----------------------------------

Freeform ‘eca’ configuration, rendered to ‘~/.config/eca/config.json’
and deep-merged over the default OpenRouter provider (later values win).
Any eca key is expressible (‘providers’, ‘models’, ‘rules’,
‘mcpServers’, ‘behavior’, ...).  Use eca's ‘${env:VAR}’ syntax for
secrets so nothing sensitive lands in the Nix store; provide the
referenced variables through ‘services.jotain.environmentFile’.

   _Type:_ JSON value

   _Default:_

{ }

   _Example:_

{
  providers.anthropic = {
    api = "anthropic";
    key = "${env:ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}";
    models."claude-sonnet-4.6" = { };
  };
}

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.14 services.jotain.environmentFile
--------------------------------------

Path to a ‘systemdexec5’
(https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/systemd.exec.html)-style
environment file (‘VAR=value’ lines) loaded into the Jotain daemon's
environment.  Every subprocess Emacs spawns inherits it, so this is the
single place to supply credentials for all external-system integrations
that read the environment -- among them gptel's ‘OPENROUTER_API_KEY’ /
‘ANTHROPIC_API_KEY’ / ‘GEMINI_API_KEY’ (lisp/init-ai.el), the ‘eca’
server's provider keys, and any token a tool reads from the environment.
Point it at a runtime secret path (sops-nix, agenix, ...); the file is
read at daemon start and never copied into the Nix store.  On Linux it
becomes the service's ‘EnvironmentFile’; on macOS the launchd agent
sources it before exec.  Secrets that instead live in a password manager
can be resolved through auth-source -- see
‘services.jotain.onePassword.enable’ and ‘services.jotain.authSources’.

   _Type:_ null or absolute path

   _Default:_

null

   _Example:_

"/run/secrets/jotain-env"

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.15 services.jotain.extraOptions
-----------------------------------

Extra command-line arguments to pass to ‘emacs’ when starting the
daemon.

   _Type:_ list of string

   _Default:_

[ ]

   _Example:_

[
  "-f"
  "exwm-enable"
]

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.16 services.jotain.nativeCompile.enable
-------------------------------------------

AOT native-compile the Jotain config into the store, so the daemon loads
‘.eln’ for ‘init.el’ and the ‘lisp/’ modules from ‘services.jotain’'s
own derivation instead of JIT-compiling into ‘var/eln-cache’ after every
deploy (every deploy that touches ‘lisp/’ moves the store path, which
invalidates the JIT cache).  ‘early-init.el’ is structurally excluded:
its ‘.eln’ lookup happens before the load path is extended, so it always
runs from ‘.elc’ -- with or without this option.

   The store-‘.eln’-through-symlinks mechanism is sound: Emacs
‘realpath()’s the source before hashing it into the ‘.eln’ name
(src/comp.c, Bug#44701), so the HM symlinks resolve to the exact path
the AOT step compiled against.  Off by default only because enabling it
adds a native-compilation pass to every activation that rebuilds the
config, and roughly 50-150 MB of ‘.eln’ to the closure.

   _Type:_ boolean

   _Default:_

false

   _Example:_

true

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.17 services.jotain.onePassword.enable
-----------------------------------------

Whether to enable the 1Password CLI (‘op’) on the wrapper PATH, the
backend for ‘auth-source-1password’ (lisp/init-systems.el) that resolves
credentials for gptel, ‘forge’, smtpmail, etc.  from the vault when they
are not supplied through ‘services.jotain.environmentFile’.  Pulls the
unfree ‘_1password-cli’ package, so it needs ‘allowUnfree’ .

   _Type:_ boolean

   _Default:_

false

   _Example:_

true

   _Declared by:_

   • modulenix (https://github.com/Jylhis/jotain/blob/main/module.nix)

A.2.18 services.jotain.shellAliases.enable
------------------------------------------

Whether to enable shell aliases for the Jotain daemon and clients.

